### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, and any new modules you add beside it under `src/`.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `GameDefinition<RefractDebugApi>`: the game
instance class, a level registry holding the single level the game runs in, and
`startLevel` naming it. The game opens that one level and never opens another;
every screen is a value of the state's `screen` field, so the world and its
game state live for the whole session. The instance's `initialize` registers
the actions, defines the cues, and returns the debug surface. The level's game
mode runs the screens and the rules, and it adds a single player in its
`beginPlay`, possessing nothing; that player's controller is where the actions
and the pointer are read. The mode's `gameStateClass` is `RefractState`, so the
world's game state is the state `specs/state.md` declares. The actors,
components, and controllers you write draw that state and drive it. As seeded,
the definition is a stub whose instance throws
`"not implemented"`. Implement it.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `RefractState` and the types it is built
from, exactly as `specs/state.md` states them, and `RefractDebugApi`, the debug
and automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` fixes.

